A||||28||Adrift||Is it possible to upgrade a 4cyl '84 up to 180-200 Horses?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||06-26-2001||05:59 PM||||Whats the cheapest / easiest / most effienct way to make a 84' 4cyl Fiero go faster? (I'd like to try to avoid dropping in a 8cyl small block). What are my options?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Adrift<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||06-26-2001||06:10 PM||||180-200 HP? Build a performance 3.4L Camaro engine up. You can get about 190-210 HP out of one with some work like Performance heads, headers, foraged pistons, 30 or 60 overbore, different cam, and bigger injectors.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||06-26-2001||06:41 PM||||How much would something like that cost?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Steve Normington||06-26-2001||07:00 PM||||Putting a 3.4 (or any V-6) in an 84 Fiero is not going to be an easy job.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||06-26-2001||08:00 PM||||For a 3.4 crate engine installed by a qualified mechanic you are looking at $4500.  Then you will want to upgrade suspension, brakes, transmission and probably other stuff.  You can get it done right for under $10K.  Or you can go to your local hack, get a junk 100000 mile 3.4, slap it in for $250.  Wiring will be extra.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mickey_Moose||06-26-2001||09:49 PM||||...not up to the info...but what exaclty was different on the engine of the Indy car (the real pace car) vs the production model? I do believe that there was a significant difference in the power of these.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierodude||06-26-2001||10:04 PM||||ive got a 3.1 for sale with 90k for $300!<P>------------------<BR>slice the rice! it dosen't pay to drive jap - crap!||||reg||12
Z||000007||fierodude||06-26-2001||10:05 PM||||the motors in the indys are the same as the regular 2.5l from what i hear there was a few 2.7l turbo 4cly indys...<P>------------------<BR>slice the rice! it dosen't pay to drive jap - crap!||||reg||6
Z||000008||KRMFiero||06-26-2001||10:06 PM||||You can easly get 180 hp out of a duke, for about 1.5 seconds on nitrus until the rods break and go through the block  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>go here: <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <P><BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||Pontiaddict||06-26-2001||10:31 PM||||The actual Indy pace cars (not the replicas the dealers sold) had 2.7l superduty engines, basically a GM race engine.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||saleenfiero||06-26-2001||10:34 PM||||I'm sure you could get 200 horse or more out of a 2.5, all you need is one soul and the right connections  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||06-26-2001||11:12 PM||||200+ HP out of an Iron Duke is entirely possible, but you're not going to mod the factory engine to that level.  You have to replace virtually EVERYTHING - block, head, crank, rods, etc. with Super Duty parts from the dealership. Very high dollar and exotic way to go, but it would be awesome.  With today's powerplants, it's just not done unless you want the uniqueness. Just drop in a 3.4 or 3800SC - much cheaper.||||reg||6
Z||000012||Oreif||06-26-2001||11:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierodude:<BR><B>the motors in the indys are the same as the regular 2.5l from what i hear there was a few 2.7l turbo 4cly indys...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The motors in the REAL Pace cars are not 2.5L Dukes. They are 2.7" SD4's. It was a performance/racing engine designed by Pontiac. It is also what was used in the IMSA Fiero race cars. The horsepower of the SD4's was 232 to almost 585 (Drag car on top fuel) The 232hp and 288hp versions were popular for street cars (daily driving) but the cost was $9800+ for just the motors.<BR>Many of the SD4 parts can be used on a stock 2.5L but the weak link in the stock 2.5L is the block itself. KRP now has SD4 engines which are still used for ARCA racing series.<BR>The smallest engine is a 218hp engine using a Chevy 202 head and a small 4bbl carb. Cost is $8800. With fuel injection you get 224hp and the cost is $10,105. These are "street legal" engines with emission controls, but are not CARB certified for use in California.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||06-27-2001||03:27 AM||||It would take an absolute miracle to get that kind of power out of a production 2.5L 4 cyl.<P>About 120-140hp is the absolute reliable limit of a production based 2.5L Iron Duke, and this is only if it's rebuilt right with the proper parts AND if the car gods like you.<P>Once you get into Super Duty 4 parts though, anything is possible....but be prepared to have to rob a bank to be able to afford the parts.<P>Anyone ever see one of these Dukes?  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.jps.net/gtracer/pont88/pm_sd4.jpg"> <P>It's a Super Duty 4 with a Cosworth 16 valve head.  A little over 300hp normally aspirated. <P>I want one...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||horseman||06-27-2001||08:35 AM||||Smokey Yunick achieved 250HP out of the duke while getting  50+MPG. The only internal mod was a camshaft change. The key was in the exhaust and induction setups. Don't take my word for it;goto Hot Rod archives and get the June'84 article titled "Smokey's New Hot Air Engine...THE NEXT STEP <BR>If I ever find the time, I will have a similar engine in my fiero. ||||reg||4
Z||000015||1FST2M6||06-27-2001||03:59 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.nitrouswarehouse.com" TARGET=_blank>www.nitrouswarehouse.com</A>  kit number 20920 and the will to go faster will put you at 200...  years ago my parents had a Glastron SSV177 with a 153 I/O OMC and it made 180hp so the boat maker claimed (and the valve cover).. it'd pull 3 skiers no problem and cruise at 80mph all day long||||reg||1
Z||000016||StuGood||06-27-2001||07:28 PM||||How to get 180 reliable horsepower out of Iron Dukes:<P>Use two.||||reg||12
Z||000017||erikred||06-27-2001||07:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>Whats the cheapest / easiest / most effienct way to make a 84' 4cyl Fiero go faster? (I'd like to try to avoid dropping in a 8cyl small block). What are my options?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Adrift</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For cheap and easy you have two options (inspite of what most people will tell you).<P>1. Get a used v6, wiring harness, and computer. You should be able to get something like 135-150 without mods.<P>2. Build up the stock duke yourself. It is not as hard as most people would try to tell you. If you put it together yourself you should be able to achieve 200hp, have decent reliability, and you can do it for less than $2k. Many performance parts are available for it still. The stock block should be able to handle 200 or more if you are not using it in extreme race situations. People like to say the block cannot handle this power, but were is the proof?<P>BTW, a used full race SD4 engine with all the expensive goodies can be had for &lt;$6k.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000018||beachbomb||06-27-2001||09:04 PM||||Just for info, the real pace car at Indy had a bored 2.7L 4 cyc, 232HP, avg speed at Indy was over 136MPH, i believe they had two trottle bodies on them and also had the air scoop on the roof..i haven't seen or read anything about a turbo being on it...Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||1
Z||000019||Brucepts||06-27-2001||09:22 PM||||Humm with all this talk about HP, why not a nice "stump pullin" little torque monster that would suckup those high rev'in 6's in a redlight dash? Or a twisty turn'y backroads burner, while they are looking for those high rpm numbers u'd be pullin' away!! Course they'd catch you on the big long straights  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Sorry my background in motors deals with pulling tractors so I'm always looking for torque not HP numbers  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> HP numbers without RPM reference is useless info in my book. For me a flat torque curve is what I'm after . . .<P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000020||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||06-28-2001||03:25 AM||||Where's the proof that a production Iron duke cannot handle 200hp?<P>In my garage.<P>The block cracked after 300 miles on my 120 to 140 HP Iron Duke build.<P>The block passed magnaflux, line bore, 0.030" overbore, comp cam, Holley TBI and manifold.<P>Iron Duke engines can be unforgiving.  I broke in the camshaft for 30 minutes at 2,000rpm.   Then the oil was changed.  Over the following 300 miles, I was careful not to drive it hard, but it did not matter.  <P>Now I am in a bad spot.  A 1984 Fiero is not easy for engine swapping.  On the other hand, this block was from a good running 90K engine that was rebuilt with car.  I am worried that getting another block will just lead to more problems.<P>A super duty block from KRP set up for the production head (smog legal) is $2500.<P>A buick V6 series II is about a $3000 swap.<P>The quad 4 is a b*tch to install.<P>I don't mind spending all my money on the Fiero, but when it does no good then I get frustrated.<P>I would say the 3800 buick V6 is the right "next move" for me.  But, it is going to be a lot of work and a lot more money.<P>If you have a good running duke, count your blessings and drive it.  Want more power?  <P>There are no easy answers.  I can say that a performance build up on the Duke is risky.  I can also say that swapping in a different engine is hard work.<P>I think though, in the end, that we forget the struggle and see only the good in our achievements.  If this is true, then trying to get 200 hp from a stock Iron Duke is the wrong move.<P>Turbo 2.8/3.1/3.4, Buick V6 3800 series II or any supercharged 3800, 3.4 TDC, Northstar, SBC, or Super Duty . . . those are the only certain ways to get 200 HP+ in your Fiero.  A quad 4 HO comes close, but may not be reliable when set up for 200HP+.<P>Sorry . . . But at least you don't have a cracked block in your driveway.  ||||reg||8
Z||000021||erikred||06-28-2001||09:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>Where's the proof that a production Iron duke cannot handle 200hp?<P>In my garage.<P>The block cracked after 300 miles on my 120 to 140 HP Iron Duke build.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry to hear that. Where did the block crack? It seems strange that 20-40 HP over stock would crack any block. I have heard of stock engines that have had no problem. In any case you could just go with a stronger production block or the Merc. block w/Fiero heads+ accessories.||||reg||11
Z||000022||kinboyatuwo||06-29-2001||04:45 PM||||GUYS GUYS GUYS <BR>you are all missing the obvious answer, <P>ADD ANOTHER DUKE<BR>how hard can it be, with some of the stuff ive seen.....<BR>and no one would have one like yours<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/kinboyatuwo/kinboyatuwo.jpg">    <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||12
Z||000023||qwikgta||06-29-2001||07:56 PM||||My duke has around 65K on it and I added a header and open exhust.  I also have a open element air cleaner.  What do you think (HP) I gained with the parts.  I do feel a differance in the torque on 2-3 and 3-4 shifts.  Did this add about 10 hp you think.<P>ROb<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000024||Adrift||06-29-2001||07:58 PM||||What about adding a turbo?<BR><P>------------------<BR>My Dream Fiero, you ask?<BR>That would be a automatic '86 or newer V6 GT Fastback with T-Tops and A/C, fully running and for sale for under $1000 CAN. Know someone who's selling a car like this for this price in the Toronto area? PLEASE E-MAIL ME!!!||||reg||1
Z||000025||tiacPaul||06-30-2001||03:54 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.users.qwest.net/~racing2win/Fiero_Fun.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.users.qwest.net/~racing2win/Fiero_Fun.htm</A> <P>This should clear up the muddy water you guys are stirring up.  ||||reg||12
Z||000026||erikred||07-02-2001||12:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>What about adding a turbo?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you want turbo on the Duke you will have turn the boost up enough to make the cost <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">erformance ratio nice. In that case you better get some SBC rods and some forged or at least hyperutechtic pistons. I am planning a turbo setup for mine this winter after I rebuild it with the good parts. The turbo can be done cheap if you use junk yard parts and do the work yourself. I am planning on having about $1000 dollars or less in to mine and at least 200hp.||||reg||14
Z||000027||Dave Gunsul||07-02-2001||01:56 PM||||No flames intended but: the reason you craked<BR>your block is more than likely the overbore.<P>You can add some power to the duke without blowing it up. <BR>I would suggest following these precautions when building ANY duke engine:<P>Honing the block with no overbore.<P>make sure to have the block checked. (magnaflux as Luckytheywere did above)<P>Get a good balancing job done.<P>Use a high vol. oil pump.<P>Prelube the engine before starting with a drill.<P>Make sure you give the engine a good break-in period. 3,000 miles or more before even thinking of getting on it!<P>Use syn. oil after the 3,000 mile breakin.<P>Make sure you use a PF52 oil filter!<P>You may also want to consider: a remote dual<BR>oil filter mount. (I read about a guy who used one of these on a homemade TWIN TURBO<BR>DUKE that had been driving on the road for a couple of years with no problems.)<BR>And/or an oil cooler.<P>Keeping all these things in mind you have to decide whether to build the engine up or bolt<BR>on a turbo.<BR>You can still buy a turbo for the 4cyl. from<BR>Fiero Conversions in Canada.<BR>They don't have a web site that i'm aware of but the phone# is: (519)-972-4989<BR>Cost is $3400 <BR><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||4
Z||000028||fotofrank||07-02-2001||05:41 PM||||I agree with Dave Gunsul (building a Duke) and BRUCEPTS (torque). The first deccision is what you want to do. Good pick up from a stop light and high top speed are two differant things. The first requires a LOT of torque and horse power and the second not so much torque and lots of hourse power. My duke,before I had a valve job and the Hooker header, got 104hp as measured on my G-Tech.That my friends is at the wheels. (a good guess is about a 15% loss through the drive train. So does that mead I 've got a 122hp Duke?)I have yet to get the "valve lash adjusted so I do not know what the present hp is. So if you have adventure,mony and a desire to do the exceptional, build up yor DUKE. Lost of fun and fofillment.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||14
